            <article class="about-story">
                <header class="about-story-header">
                    <h1>How I began to recognize God in the middle of an uncertain life</h1>
                    <p class="story-location">A personal testimony from Southern Leyte</p>
                    <p class="about-lede">Kristohanon grew from a season when I was trying to survive one day at a time—and from the quiet way Scripture taught me that God had not abandoned me.</p>
                </header>

                <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="uncertainDays">
                    <h2 id="uncertainDays">When every day felt uncertain</h2>
                    <p>I am a Catholic living in Southern Leyte. Like many people, I wanted only a stable life: enough work to meet my responsibilities, enough income to face the next bill, and enough peace to sleep without wondering what tomorrow would take from me.</p>
                    <p>Becoming a virtual assistant was supposed to make life feel more secure. I was grateful to work, but financial pressure did not simply disappear. There were days when I was earning and still felt as though I was barely moving. I kept calculating, worrying, comparing, and asking myself why hard work had not yet brought the stability I imagined.</p>
                    <p>From the outside, I was functioning. Inside, I was tired. I feared losing what little I had, and I carried the painful feeling that my life was standing still while everyone else was moving forward.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="surrender">
                    <h2 id="surrender">The moment I stopped trying to control everything</h2>
                    <p>Eventually I reached a point where I could no longer carry every fear by myself. Giving everything to God did not mean abandoning my responsibilities or waiting passively for life to change. It meant admitting that control had become my false source of security.</p>
                    <p>I began to tell God honestly that I was afraid. I placed before Him my work, my finances, my plans, and even the future I thought I needed. I did not receive an instant solution. What changed first was quieter: I began to make room for God in the uncertainty.</p>
                    <p>Surrender became a daily choice. Some days it was peaceful. Other days I had to surrender the same fear again. But little by little, prayer stopped being the last thing I tried after worrying. It became the place where I started.</p>
                </section>

                <blockquote class="story-callout"><p>I thought I needed God to change my circumstances before I could have peace. Scripture taught me that God could meet me inside those circumstances—and change the way I carried them.</p></blockquote>

                <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="scripture">
                    <h2 id="scripture">God met me through the Scriptures I encountered</h2>
                    <p>I started noticing Bible passages wherever I found them. Sometimes a verse appeared at the right moment. Sometimes I returned to a familiar passage and understood it differently because of what I was living through. I began to slow down, read carefully, and ask what those words revealed about God—not only what they might give me.</p>
                    <p>The more I read, the more I recognized a God who was close to people in fear, hunger, grief, waiting, and doubt. Scripture did not pretend that suffering was easy. It showed me that difficulty was not proof of God’s absence. It reminded me that my worth was not measured by my income, that I was not alone in waiting, and that hope could exist before an answer arrived.</p>
                    <p>I did not become perfect or suddenly understand everything. I simply became a little closer to God. That small closeness changed the direction of my days. I became more attentive, more grateful, and less ruled by fear. The problems were still real, but they no longer had the final word.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="doors">
                    <h2 id="doors">Then doors began to open</h2>
                    <p>In that season, opportunities began arriving in ways I had not planned. Even when I was not actively submitting job applications, people and possibilities came into my life. I received them as grace—not as a reward I had earned for praying correctly, and not as proof that God promises every believer financial success.</p>
                    <p>The deeper miracle was not simply that work appeared. It was that I could recognize provision without making money my god. I could receive an opportunity with gratitude rather than desperation. I could work hard without believing that everything depended on my strength alone.</p>
                    <p>There are still uncertain days. Faith has not removed every struggle. But I now know where to return when fear becomes loud: to God, to prayer, and to Scripture that reminds me who He is.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="invitation">
                    <h2 id="invitation">Why I want you to experience this too</h2>
                    <p>Perhaps your life feels stagnant. You may be working without seeing progress, praying without feeling an answer, or carrying a burden that no one around you fully understands. I cannot promise that every circumstance will change quickly. Kristohanon cannot promise a job, wealth, healing, or a life without pain.</p>
                    <p>What I can share is the invitation that changed me: let God be first. Bring Him the truth of what you carry. Read Scripture slowly enough to understand it. Return tomorrow even if today feels ordinary. Allow His Word to form your desires, steady your fear, and teach you to recognize grace.</p>
                    <p>Peace does not always arrive because the world becomes easy. Sometimes it arrives because, in the middle of the same world, you discover that you are held by God. Happiness becomes less about having complete control and more about knowing where your life is rooted.</p>
                    <p>That is why I created Kristohanon. I want this to be a place where someone who does not know what passage to find can still begin; where English and Cebuano speakers can meet Scripture in words they understand; and where technology quietly points beyond itself toward Christ.</p>
                </section>

                <footer class="story-ending"><p>Amid the waiting, there is a way forward. Begin with God. Bring Him this day, then the next.</p><a class="story-action" href="/#share">Share what is on your heart</a></footer>
            </article>

            <aside class="about-trust" aria-labelledby="trustTitle">
                <h2 id="trustTitle">Scripture first, technology second</h2>
                <p>Kristohanon retrieves reviewed passages before composing a reflection. Scripture text is displayed from a verified source rather than generated from memory.</p>
                <p>This is a Christ-centered reflection tool, not Jesus himself, divine revelation, clergy, therapy, or emergency care.</p>
